  "conclusions": {
    "single_query": "True pairs co-fire at 15.56% vs near-misses at 10.81% (1.44x). The gap is real but narrow — near-misses fire at ~70% the rate of true pairs.",
    "reinforcement": "Hebbian accumulation DOES NOT sharpen the gradient. After 10 sessions of reinforcement per theme, the edgeStore bonus for near-misses converges to 1.00x of true pairs. The initial advantage is erased because near-misses appear in top-k at 10-15% rate during theme-member queries, and reinforceRecall() reinforces all pairs in top-k indiscriminately.",
    "verdict": "RM's associative bet is NOT validated by nomic embeddings. Neither single-query cosine similarity nor accumulated Hebbian reinforcement separates true thematic pairs from semantically-close near-misses. The near-miss records that co-fire highly share genuine semantic overlap (names, professions, furniture/DIY, exercise/fitness), and nomic-embed-text-v1.5 maps them close enough that they get reinforced at nearly the same rate as true pairs.",
    "implication": "The associative layer's Related: section will contain near-misses at near-equal bonus levels to true pairs. This means the 'Related:' block is not a reliable signal of genuine thematic connection — it's a gradient where true pairs have only a marginal edge. The system may benefit from: (1) higher-dimensional embeddings, (2) query-aware weighting (not all top-k records should reinforce equally), (3) temporal decay that differentiates recent co-activation from stale co-activation, or (4) a threshold on the Hebbian bonus that only activates near-miss edges when the bonus gap is large enough."
